**Read:** [Advanced fluid dynamics — Mei notes and MIT modules](../sources/Advanced-Fluid-Dynamics-Mei-MIT.lingua#r_21NXVH78F0NYDPDVTG66PG261P), rotating-fluid module and chapter 7.a; [Fluid Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA), chapter 4 rotating shallow-water motion; [Fluid mechanics films and illustrated notes — NCFMF](../sources/NCFMF-Fluid-Mechanics-Films.lingua#r_0RT6J26HJJ83MX74030JPEHQVP), rotating-flow demonstrations.

In a frame rotating steadily with angular velocity Ω, the momentum equation contains Coriolis acceleration 2Ω×u and centrifugal effects. A time-varying rotation adds another term. Centrifugal acceleration may be incorporated into an effective potential in appropriate circumstances, but that bookkeeping does not remove all rotational physics.

Compare advective acceleration with Coriolis acceleration using a declared Rossby number. In an appropriate slow, large-scale horizontal balance, pressure gradient and Coriolis force produce geostrophic motion. That leading balance leaves questions about ageostrophic flow, boundary layers, acceleration and mass continuity to higher-order or additional equations.

Derive an Ekman-layer response for a specified wall or surface stress and connect its transport to the interior. State rotation direction, coordinates and boundary conditions before assigning transport direction. Use the comparison to explain how a small frictional region can control a larger circulation and why rotating laboratory models require more than a matched Reynolds number.

